  "notes": "West leads the ♦J against 5♣. West's lead should be won in dummy. South's only possible losers are in the spade suit. By playing for an elimination, South can be sure of holding his spade losers to two.\n\nA diamond is ruffed at trick 2. South draws trump, ending in dummy, and ruffs dummy's last diamond. Three rounds of hearts are played, ending in dummy.\n\nDiamonds and hearts are now eliminated from the North and South hands. A spade is played from the dummy. If East follows low, the ♠10 is played from the closed hand. West can win cheaply, but he will be endplayed. A spade return will make South's ♠K good. A heart or diamond return will concede a ruff and discard.\n\nIf East follows with the ♠J or ♠Q, South covers with the ♠K. West can win the ♠A, but he will be endplayed as before. West will either have to set up the ♠10 or concede a ruff and discard.",
